{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And Pharaoh rose up in the night.</b> The reason for its saying <b>in the night</b> is explained according to the statement of the blessed memory of Amram (Zohar 2:38a), for that night was as bright as day, illuminating like the brightness of day. And I alluded to this hint in the verse (Exodus 13:8) ״וְהִגַּדְתָּ לְבִנְךָ בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא״ — for it intends to say that he should tell him also about the miracle of the night, which became day. Now perhaps you will say that light shone in the night for the wicked and the good alike; therefore it says <b>and Pharaoh rose up in the night</b> — when he rose to call for Moses and Aaron, HaShem withheld its light from the wicked one, and it was night, meaning darkness, as it says (Genesis 1:5) ״וְלַחֹשֶׁךְ קָרָא לָיְלָה״."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:15.434812661Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}